{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'same|lease|VerifyLeader|stale|timeout' ../native-source/config.go ../native-source/docs/*.md ../native-source/README.md; sed -n '135,211p' ../native-source/config.go; sed -n '965,987p' ../native-source/raft.go; sed -n '100,113p' ../native-source/replication.go; sed -n '274,296p' ../native-source/future.go; sed -n '880,894p' ../native-source/api.go\"","aggregated_output":")\n\n// Config provides any necessary configuration for the Raft server.\ntype Config struct {\n\t// ProtocolVersion allows a Raft server to inter-operate with older\n\t// Raft servers running an older version of the code. This is used to\n\t// version the wire protocol as well as Raft-specific log entries that\n\t// the server uses when _speaking_ to other servers. There is currently\n\t// no auto-negotiation of versions so all servers must be manually\n\t// configured with compatible versions. See ProtocolVersionMin and\n\t// ProtocolVersionMax for the versions of the protocol that this server\n\t// can _understand_.\n\tProtocolVersion ProtocolVersion\n\n\t// HeartbeatTimeout specifies the time in follower state without contact\n\t// from a leader before we attempt an election.\n\tHeartbeatTimeout time.Duration\n\n\t// ElectionTimeout specifies the time in candidate state without contact\n\t// from a leader before we attempt an election.\n\tElectionTimeout time.Duration\n\n\t// CommitTimeout specifies the time without an Apply operation before the\n\t// leader sends an AppendEntry RPC to followers, to ensure a timely commit of\n\t// log entries.\n\t// Due to random staggering, may be delayed as much as 2x this value.\n\tCommitTimeout time.Duration\n\n\t// MaxAppendEntries controls the maximum number of append entries\n\t// to send at once. We want to strike a balance between efficiency\n\t// and avoiding waste if the follower is going to reject because of\n\t// an inconsistent log.\n\tMaxAppendEntries int\n\n\t// BatchApplyCh indicates whether we should buffer applyCh\n\t// to size MaxAppendEntries. This enables batch log commitment,\n\t// but breaks the timeout guarantee on Apply. Specifically,\n\t// a log can be added to the applyCh buffer but not actually be\n\t// processed until after the specified timeout.\n\tBatchApplyCh bool\n\n\t// If we are a member of a cluster, and RemovePeer is invoked for the\n\t// local node, then we forget all peers and transition into the follower state.\n\t// If ShutdownOnRemove is set, we additional shutdown Raft. Otherwise,\n\t// we can become a leader of a cluster containing only this node.\n\tShutdownOnRemove bool\n\n\t// TrailingLogs controls how many logs we leave after a snapshot. This is used\n\t// so that we can quickly replay logs on a follower instead of being forced to\n\t// send an entire snapshot. The value passed here is the initial setting used.\n\t// This can be tuned during operation using ReloadConfig.\n\tTrailingLogs uint64\n\n\t// SnapshotInterval controls how often we check if we should perform a\n\t// snapshot. We randomly stagger between this value and 2x this value to avoid\n\t// the entire cluster from performing a snapshot at once. The value passed\n\t// here is the initial setting used. This can be tuned during operation using\n\t// ReloadConfig.\n\tSnapshotInterval time.Duration\n\n\t// SnapshotThreshold controls how many outstanding logs there must be before\n\t// we perform a snapshot. This is to prevent excessive snapshotting by\n\t// replaying a small set of logs instead. The value passed here is the initial\n\t// setting used. This can be tuned during operation using ReloadConfig.\n\tSnapshotThreshold uint64\n\n\t// LeaderLeaseTimeout is used to control how long the \"lease\" lasts\n\t// for being the leader without being able to contact a quorum\n\t// of nodes. If we reach this interval without contact, we will\n\t// step down as leader.\n\tLeaderLeaseTimeout time.Duration\n\n\t// LocalID is a unique ID for this server across all time. When running with\n\t// ProtocolVersion < 3, you must set this to be the same as the network\n\t// address of your transport.\n\tLocalID ServerID\n\nfunc (r *Raft) verifyLeader(v *verifyFuture) {\n\t// Current leader always votes for self\n\tv.votes = 1\n\n\t// Set the quorum size, hot-path for single node\n\tv.quorumSize = r.quorumSize()\n\tif v.quorumSize == 1 {\n\t\tv.respond(nil)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\n\t// Track this request\n\tv.notifyCh = r.verifyCh\n\tr.leaderState.notify[v] = struct{}{}\n\n\t// Trigger immediate heartbeats\n\tfor _, repl := range r.leaderState.replState {\n\t\trepl.notifyLock.Lock()\n\t\trepl.notify[v] = struct{}{}\n\t\trepl.notifyLock.Unlock()\n\t\tasyncNotifyCh(repl.notifyCh)\n\t}\n}\nfunc (s *followerReplication) notifyAll(leader bool) {\n\t// Clear the waiting notifies minimizing lock time\n\ts.notifyLock.Lock()\n\tn := s.notify\n\ts.notify = make(map[*verifyFuture]struct{})\n\ts.notifyLock.Unlock()\n\n\t// Submit our votes\n\tfor v := range n {\n\t\tv.vote(leader)\n\t}\n}\n\n// cleanNotify is used to delete notify, .\nfunc (v *verifyFuture) vote(leader bool) {\n\tv.voteLock.Lock()\n\tdefer v.voteLock.Unlock()\n\n\t// Guard against having notified already\n\tif v.notifyCh == nil {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\n\tif leader {\n\t\tv.votes++\n\t\tif v.votes >= v.quorumSize {\n\t\t\tv.notifyCh <- v\n\t\t\tv.notifyCh = nil\n\t\t}\n\t} else {\n\t\tv.notifyCh <- v\n\t\tv.notifyCh = nil\n\t}\n}\n\n// appendFuture is used for waiting on a pipelined append\n// entries RPC.\n// VerifyLeader is used to ensure this peer is still the leader. It may be used\n// to prevent returning stale data from the FSM after the peer has lost\n// leadership.\nfunc (r *Raft) VerifyLeader() Future {\n\tmetrics.IncrCounter([]string{\"raft\", \"verify_leader\"}, 1)\n\tverifyFuture := &verifyFuture{}\n\tverifyFuture.init()\n\tselect {\n\tcase <-r.shutdownCh:\n\t\treturn errorFuture{ErrRaftShutdown}\n\tcase r.verifyCh <- verifyFuture:\n\t\treturn verifyFuture\n\t}\n}\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '35,54p' ../native-source/docs/README.md; sed -n '68,84p' ../native-source/README.md; sed -n '990,1031p' ../native-source/raft.go; sed -n '135,202p' ../native-source/replication.go\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '35,54p' ../native-source/docs/README.md; sed -n '68,84p' ../native-source/README.md; sed -n '990,1031p' ../native-source/raft.go; sed -n '135,202p' ../native-source/replication.go\"","aggregated_output":"* **FSM** - finite state machine, stores the cluster state\n* **Client** - the application that uses the `hashicorp/raft` library\n\n## Operations\n\n### Leader Write\n\nMost write operations must be performed on the leader.\n\n* RequestConfigChange - update the raft peer list configuration\n* Apply - apply a log entry to the log on a majority of peers, and the FSM. See [raft apply](apply.md) for more details.\n* Barrier - a special Apply that does not modify the FSM, used to wait for previous logs to be applied\n* LeadershipTransfer - stop accepting client requests, and tell a different peer to start a leadership election\n* Restore (Snapshot) - overwrite the cluster state with the contents of the snapshot (excluding cluster configuration)\n* VerifyLeader - send a heartbeat to all voters to confirm the peer is still the leader\n\n### Follower Write\n\n* BootstrapCluster - store the cluster configuration in the local log store\n\n### Protocol Description\n\nRaft nodes are always in one of three states: follower, candidate or leader. All\nnodes initially start out as a follower. In this state, nodes can accept log entries\nfrom a leader and cast votes. If no entries are received for some time, nodes\nself-promote to the candidate state. In the candidate state nodes request votes from\ntheir peers. If a candidate receives a quorum of votes, then it is promoted to a leader.\nThe leader must accept new log entries and replicate to all the other followers.\nIn addition, if stale reads are not acceptable, all queries must also be performed on\nthe leader.\n\nOnce a cluster has a leader, it is able to accept new log entries. A client can\nrequest that a leader append a new log entry, which is an opaque binary blob to\nRaft. The leader then writes the entry to durable storage and attempts to replicate\nto a quorum of followers. Once the log entry is considered *committed*, it can be\n*applied* to a finite state machine. The finite state machine is application specific,\nand is implemented using an interface.\nfunc (r *Raft) leadershipTransfer(id ServerID, address ServerAddress, repl *followerReplication, stopCh chan struct{}, doneCh chan error) {\n\t// make sure we are not already stopped\n\tselect {\n\tcase <-stopCh:\n\t\tdoneCh <- nil\n\t\treturn\n\tdefault:\n\t}\n\n\tfor atomic.LoadUint64(&repl.nextIndex) <= r.getLastIndex() {\n\t\terr := &deferError{}\n\t\terr.init()\n\t\trepl.triggerDeferErrorCh <- err\n\t\tselect {\n\t\tcase err := <-err.errCh:\n\t\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\t\tdoneCh <- err\n\t\t\t\treturn\n\t\t\t}\n\t\tcase <-stopCh:\n\t\t\tdoneCh <- nil\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// Step ?: the thesis describes in chap 6.4.1: Using clocks to reduce\n\t// messaging for read-only queries. If this is implemented, the lease\n\t// has to be reset as well, in case leadership is transferred. This\n\t// implementation also has a lease, but it serves another purpose and\n\t// doesn't need to be reset. The lease mechanism in our raft lib, is\n\t// setup in a similar way to the one in the thesis, but in practice\n\t// it's a timer that just tells the leader how often to check\n\t// heartbeats are still coming in.\n\n\t// Step 3: send TimeoutNow message to target server.\n\terr := r.trans.TimeoutNow(id, address, &TimeoutNowRequest{RPCHeader: r.getRPCHeader()}, &TimeoutNowResponse{})\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\terr = fmt.Errorf(\"failed to make TimeoutNow RPC to %v: %v\", id, err)\n\t}\n\tdoneCh <- err\n}\n\n// replicate is a long running routine that replicates log entries to a single\n// follower.\nfunc (r *Raft) replicate(s *followerReplication) {\n\t// Start an async heartbeating routing\n\tstopHeartbeat := make(chan struct{})\n\tdefer close(stopHeartbeat)\n\tr.goFunc(func() { r.heartbeat(s, stopHeartbeat) })\n\nRPC:\n\tshouldStop := false\n\tfor !shouldStop {\n\t\tselect {\n\t\tcase maxIndex := <-s.stopCh:\n\t\t\t// Make a best effort to replicate up to this index\n\t\t\tif maxIndex > 0 {\n\t\t\t\tr.replicateTo(s, maxIndex)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\tcase deferErr := <-s.triggerDeferErrorCh:\n\t\t\tlastLogIdx, _ := r.getLastLog()\n\t\t\tshouldStop = r.replicateTo(s, lastLogIdx)\n\t\t\tif !shouldStop {\n\t\t\t\tdeferErr.respond(nil)\n\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\tdeferErr.respond(fmt.Errorf(\"replication failed\"))\n\t\t\t}\n\t\tcase <-s.triggerCh:\n\t\t\tlastLogIdx, _ := r.getLastLog()\n\t\t\tshouldStop = r.replicateTo(s, lastLogIdx)\n\t\t// This is _not_ our heartbeat mechanism but is to ensure\n\t\t// followers quickly learn the leader's commit index when\n\t\t// raft commits stop flowing naturally. The actual heartbeats\n\t\t// can't do this to keep them unblocked by disk IO on the\n\t\t// follower. See https://github.com/hashicorp/raft/issues/282.\n\t\tcase <-randomTimeout(r.config().CommitTimeout):\n\t\t\tlastLogIdx, _ := r.getLastLog()\n\t\t\tshouldStop = r.replicateTo(s, lastLogIdx)\n\t\t}\n\n\t\t// If things looks healthy, switch to pipeline mode\n\t\tif !shouldStop && s.allowPipeline {\n\t\t\tgoto PIPELINE\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn\n\nPIPELINE:\n\t// Disable until re-enabled\n\ts.allowPipeline = false\n\n\t// Replicates using a pipeline for high performance. This method\n\t// is not able to gracefully recover from errors, and so we fall back\n\t// to standard mode on failure.\n\tif err := r.pipelineReplicate(s); err != nil {\n\t\tif err != ErrPipelineReplicationNotSupported {\n\t\t\ts.peerLock.RLock()\n\t\t\tpeer := s.peer\n\t\t\ts.peerLock.RUnlock()\n\t\t\tr.logger.Error(\"failed to start pipeline replication to\", \"peer\", peer, \"error\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tgoto RPC\n}\n\n// replicateTo is a helper to replicate(), used to replicate the logs up to a\n// given last index.\n// If the follower log is behind, we take care to bring them up to date.\nfunc (r *Raft) replicateTo(s *followerReplication, lastIndex uint64) (shouldStop bool) {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"The trusted run recorded a term-3 replacement leader applying index 8 before the term-2 node’s verification began. That verification returned nil without forced shutdown. The checker matched the invocation and result with no parsing gaps. I’m reviewing this as a bounded VerifyLeader contract violation; it does not establish conflicting committed logs or a stale application read.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nartifact='519e1b7b7bdc4d2ca2774233ed412354';candidate='70da42f9dbe34c69969c852e145c833e'\ndef item(aspect,sources,rationale,limits):return dict(target_id=artifact,aspect=aspect,status='no_issue_found',source_ids=sources,rationale=rationale,counterevidence=[],limitations=limits,challenged_components=[])\nitems=[item('applicability',['i_verify','i_future','i_suffrage','d_verify_voters','i_timeout_semantics','s_config_timeouts','s_membership_api','s_transport_disconnect','s_election','s_campaign'],\n 'The API contract in i_verify promises to ensure the peer is still leader and explicitly mentions prevention of stale reads after leadership loss; i_future identifies Error as completion status. d_verify_voters separately describes contacting voters. The harness uses ordinary public APIs and actual nodes, with static final membership of three voters and one nonvoter throughout the selected interval. Membership is not assumed from constructor validation: AddVoter/AddNonvoter and an applied prefix complete, then each node is polled for final latest membership and a commit index at least the prefix. Timeout comments define local election and loss-of-contact intervals; neither those comments nor ValidateConfig impose equal durations across peers. Per-node configured inequalities are satisfied. No synthetic vote, term, role, append success or recovered state is injected. Disconnect partitions are implemented by removing real transport routes. The lower-term node remaining locally Leader cannot discharge the independent public verification contract after actual higher-term election. The selected authority-loss antecedent is established by that election and the new command callback, not by missing voter responses alone.',\n ['Scope is this mixed-membership, non-pipelined in-memory deployment with unequal but valid local timeouts; no exhaustive deployment coverage.','Application read consistency is motivation from the contract, not an observed read endpoint.']),\n item('decomposition',['i_verify','i_future','s_verify','s_notify','s_future','s_loop','s_lease','s_commit','s_campaign','s_fsm','s_match','s_heartbeat'],\n 'The requirement is a necessary exclusion: established supersession with no reacquisition implies no successful verification. It does not require eventual success, exact fresh-response counts, or nil-versus-error equality to a reachability predicate. A real election is selected among the other configured voters, with higher term, followed by ApplyFuture completion and locked observation of the unique replacement-command in that node\\\\'s FSM. The partition remains unchanged, the old node has only itself plus a nonvoter, and its term remains 2 through the actual result, ruling out a new real election at that endpoint. The old lease could step down or a newer-term/negative response could reject verification; these protections are allowed to run and would satisfy the assertion. They do not explain away the observed success at term 2 after term-3 application. Commitment has a separate voter filter and is not implicated by this witness. Exact attribution to a particular heartbeat versus ordinary append notification is unnecessary for the authority-contract proposition; source registration and tally show how nonvoter notifications can participate.',\n ['The checker would not catch a stale read caused by an application bypassing VerifyLeader, nor all verification problems outside its admitted supersession antecedent.','Response counters alone do not identify the exact notifyAll call; retained as mechanism diagnostics, not the normative oracle.']),\n item('checker_correspondence',['i_verify','i_future','s_verify','s_loop','s_notify','s_future','s_inmem_transport_probe','s_transport_disconnect','s_mockfsm','s_config_timeouts','s_membership_api','s_start','s_bootstrap','s_election','s_campaign','s_commit','s_fsm','s_replication_loop'],\n 'Reviewed the fixed plan.json and assurance_generated_test.go for artifact 519e1b7b7bdc4d2ca2774233ed412354, its CheckRun 1d135dea815c487cadfbd26b759bf129 raw output, and the saved assessment. The wrapper delegates AppendEntries to real InmemTransport and only counts successful responses; optional pipeline refusal selects the existing fallback in s_replication_loop. All protocol and FSM workers start through NewRaft. Control verification completed while connected. The fixed input emits superseding_command only after a real higher-term leader, successful ApplyFuture, and actual unique command in MockFSM; invocation follows in program order. Prerequisite alias invocation explicitly references superseded terms, and the monitor selects invocation as admission_alias. operation is the stable identity shared by both prerequisites and the independent result; success is measured as result==nil from the one future, never used for association. The admission marker immediately precedes the public call; an absent return would leave no result rather than imply success. Result assertion success==false faithfully excludes nil completion for the established antecedent. Shutdown after 700ms would allow a pending operation to finish with error, not misclassify it as failed liveness. In the actual execution shutdown_forced=false: term-3 authority-2 applied index 8; the old node then admitted verification at term 2 and returned nil at term 2. Counters for remote voters stayed 6 and 4; nonvoter rose 16 to 17. Assessment found prerequisites at indices 2 and 3, result at 4, no parsing errors, and complete correlation. The Go process PASS means the harness completed; the separate property comparison is violated. This is one nonvacuous, bounded witness, not proof of general checker equivalence.',\n ['No raw vote trace is individually asserted, but the real runCandidate producer, higher-term Leader state, successful application and election log establish the selected prefix in this execution.','The connected control is executed and checked by the harness, outside the supersession monitor.','The actual violating operation completed before harness shutdown; bounded pending behavior in other schedules would establish no general liveness result.'])]\ns=dict(action='review',artifact_id=artifact,review_items=items,sources=[dict(id='d_verify_voters',file='docs/README.md',start_line=39,end_line=49,kind='document_statement'),dict(id='i_timeout_semantics',file='config.go',start_line=137,end_line=205,kind='interface_statement'),dict(id='s_replication_loop',file='replication.go',start_line=137,end_line=199,kind='code_observation')],rationale='Whole-artifact semantic reverse review of the fixed checker and actual trusted output. No counterevidence was found within the stated applicability, decomposition and correspondence scope. The completed comparison supports the bounded public verification contract violation, while application reads and log safety remain outside its conclusion.',feedback=dict(ref_ids=[artifact,'1d135dea815c487cadfbd26b759bf129',candidate],answered='The trusted same-execution check established a real term-3 leader and applied command before a term-2 isolated node\\\\'s verification invocation, then measured nil completion without forced shutdown. This replaces the initial-prefix and authority-loss gaps of the earlier exploration.',remaining=['Exact heartbeat versus ordinary-append notification attribution is not resolved by peer response counts.','No application stale-read endpoint, log inconsistency, or all-configuration theorem is established.'],understanding='unchanged',rationale='The accepted map already records the responsible producers, tally and protections. Preserve this execution in its artifact rather than turning the result into a universal map Fact. After review, compare the independent heartbeat response-term/contact path against membership-change verification registration and pa